---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureIWorkToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureIWorkToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="numbers-to-pdf">
  <PdfSecureIWorkToPdf client:load kind="numbers" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Reading a .numbers file on a PC</h2>
      <p>
        Apple's Numbers is the least portable of the three iWork formats. Excel and LibreOffice cannot
        open it, and unlike a Word document it has no widely used reader. But Numbers, like Pages and
        Keynote, writes a complete PDF rendering of the document into the file each time it saves, and
        all devices open PDF. This Numbers to PDF tool extracts it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <p>
        The document as Numbers would print it: tables with their formatting, charts as vectors,
        headers and footers, one PDF page per printed page. All sheets in the print layout appear, in
        order.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result is <strong>a document, not data</strong>. You can read it, search it, print it and
        quote from it. You cannot recalculate it, sort it or add a column.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Numbers vs Excel, structurally</h2>
      <p>
        Numbers organises a file differently from Excel, so a clean conversion to .xlsx is harder than
        it sounds:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Numbers</th>
              <th scope="col">Excel</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">A sheet is</th>
              <td>A canvas holding several independent tables, charts and text boxes</td>
              <td>One grid</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">A table is</th>
              <td>An object with its own coordinate space and header rows</td>
              <td>A region of the grid</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Cell references</th>
              <td>Often by header name</td>
              <td>By grid coordinate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Storage format</th>
              <td>Apple's IWA (a Protobuf variant), undocumented</td>
              <td>OOXML, an ISO standard</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Three tables side by side on one Numbers canvas have no single correct form as an Excel sheet.
        The printed PDF is exactly what the author intended you to see.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>If you need the numbers as data</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Best: ask the sender for File → Export To → Excel or CSV from Numbers. It takes them ten
          seconds and gives a real spreadsheet.
        </li>
        <li>
          Otherwise: convert here, then run the result through <a href="/pdf-to-excel">PDF to Excel</a>.
          It recovers the values from clusters of text positions, which works well on a plain table and
          badly on merged cells or charts. Check the totals before you rely on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          For a small table: select the text in the PDF and paste it into a spreadsheet.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Charts come through properly</h2>
      <p>
        Numbers renders charts into the preview as vector graphics, not screenshots. Bar charts, pie
        charts, scatter plots, sparklines and 2-D and 3-D chart types stay sharp at any zoom level and
        print at full resolution. This is a clear advantage of Apple's own rendering over a redrawn
        document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Print layout decides what appears</h2>
      <p>
        The preview follows Numbers' print view, so:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Sheets excluded from printing are not in the preview, and this tool cannot recover them.
        </li>
        <li>
          Page breaks, scale-to-fit and orientation are as the author set them in the print pane.
        </li>
        <li>
          A very wide table may be split across pages exactly as it would be on paper.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Batches and privacy</h2>
      <p>
        If you are moving away from a Mac, drop a whole folder. The tool processes each file in turn,
        and you can download them one by one or as one zip. Spreadsheets hold budgets, client lists,
        invoices and payroll. JavaScript in this tab opens the archive, and the PDF goes to your
        downloads folder.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same technique handles <a href="/pages-to-pdf">Pages documents</a> and
        <a href="/keynote-to-pdf">Keynote presentations</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
